== Lecture 1 – Introduction to Digital Methods and Data Skills ==

=== Data in the Wild ===

Data surrounds us in modern life. Every interaction online — whether using social media platforms, tracking your fitness through an app, or purchasing items from an e-commerce site — generates data. These data points are collected, stored, and analyzed, often invisibly, yet they inform decisions that affect both individuals and organizations. For researchers and practitioners in psychology, understanding how data is generated and used is crucial.

Data literacy allows us to critically interpret these digital traces. It involves the ability to read, understand, create, and communicate data effectively. Much like traditional literacy enables reading and writing, data literacy equips individuals to engage with information thoughtfully and responsibly. For research scientists, data literacy is essential to design studies, analyze results, and draw meaningful conclusions. For practicing psychologists, it is equally important to interpret findings reported in primary literature and apply them to clinical or organizational practice. In short, data literacy is “skepticism with a spreadsheet.”


[activity:mcq]
prompt: Which of the following is an example of early mechanized data storage?
wrong: Papyrus scrolls
wrong: Writing by hand
correct: Punched cards in Jacquard looms
correct: Tabulating machines for census data
