[end activity]

=== Data is Old ===

The practice of collecting and recording data is far from new. Ancient civilizations, such as the Babylonians, maintained detailed accounting tablets. For instance, the British Museum houses a tablet (BM 085232, P368686) excavated in Larsa, dating to the Old Babylonian period (ca. 1900–1600 BC), that records detailed accounts of grain distribution. Similarly, Francis Galton, in the late 19th century, pioneered statistical methods in anthropometry, collecting data on human traits at the 1884 International Health Exhibition.

Even before modern computers, data visualization was recognized as a powerful tool. John Snow’s 1854 cholera map demonstrated clusters of cases in London, illustrating the value of spatial analysis [cite:tulchinsky2018john]. Florence Nightingale famously used infographics to depict mortality causes in the Crimean War, effectively communicating complex statistics to policymakers[cite:bradshaw2017florence]. Charles Minard’s graphic of Napoleon’s invasion of Russia remains a benchmark for the effective communication of multivariate data [cite:moore2008cruel].

