=== Data Storage ===

From ancient papyrus scrolls to punched cards, humans have always sought methods to record and preserve information. Scribes in ancient Egypt were depicted seated on the floor with papyrus on their laps, meticulously copying records. By the 14th century, illustrations show the demon Titivillus “introducing errors” into scribes’ work, a reminder of human fallibility in data management.

The invention of the printing press by Johannes Gutenberg around 1436 revolutionized information dissemination. Later, Jacquard looms used punched cards to control weaving patterns, demonstrating early mechanized data storage. Herman Hollerith’s tabulating machine processed punched cards for the 1890 U.S. Census, illustrating how automation can accelerate data summarization. Even phonograph cylinders were used to record folk songs in the early 20th century by Béla Bartók and Zoltán Kodály, preserving cultural data that would have otherwise been lost.

![Hollerith Tabulating Machine](assets/lecture-01/tabulating_machine.jpg "Herman Hollerith's tabulating machine for the 1890 U.S. Census")

